  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 221 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe weather pattern across the region will gradually start to change \nheading into the second half of the weekend and into Monday as mid \nlevel ridging in the western Atlantic expands over South Florida and \nthen into the eastern Gulf during this time frame. At the same time, \nthe remnants of Isaias will rapidly move northeastward across the\nMidwest and into the Mid-Atlantic states on Sunday before \nstarting to push off the Mid-Atlantic Coastline on Sunday night \ninto Monday. Mid level wind flow across South Florida will \ngradually begin to shift and become more westerly on Sunday and \ninto most of Monday. This will introduce some drier air working \ninto the mid levels especially on Sunday as subsidence enhances \nacross the region due to the ridging aloft.\n\nAt the surface, southeasterly winds will gradually weaken on Sunday \nand become more easterly as the day progresses. The frontal \nboundary associated with the remnant surface low of Isaias will \nalso remain draped over Central Florida during this time frame. \nWhile there will be enough moisture in the lower levels to support\nconvective development, the highest chance will remain confined \nto the interior and Southwest Florida during the afternoon and \nevening hours with southeasterly surface wind flow in place. While\nstrong thunderstorm development will remain rather limited, an \nisolated strong storm or two cannot be completely ruled out, \npotentially producing gusty winds and heavy downpours. High \ntemperatures on Sunday will generally rise into the lower 90s \nacross most areas. Heading into Monday, the aforementioned \nfrontal boundary will slowly push across South Florida, however, \nit will not have a lot of moisture to work with at this time as it\nwill be in a weakening state, and the best dynamics will be well \nto the northeast of the region. With some lower level moisture \nlingering over the area, some isolated to scattered shower and \nthunderstorm activity will be possible as the front pushes \nthrough. High temperatures on Monday will rise into the upper 80s \nalong the east coast to the lower 90s across interior portions of \nSouthwest Florida. \n\nHeading towards Tuesday and Wednesday, a much drier air mass will \npush into the region behind the front as high pressure builds \nback into the area. This will keep chances of showers and storms \nextremely limited during this time frame, and with the drier air \nand lower dewpoints anticipated, there will be a noticeable drop \nin humidity during the middle of the week. High temperatures on \nTuesday and Wednesday will still remain above climatological\nnormals for this time of year.",
